//@version=3
// PROVENANCE: written 2026-11-15 for the Pine parity programme (legacy semantics B3, G7 ytc bar 46).
// Question: what does `high[k]` inside a USER FUNCTION read when the function is called only in an
// `if` block? Three readings, each named by its column's value on a bar the block runs (every 3rd bar):
//   EVERY BAR  high[k] is the chart's (a = high[2] of this bar)
//   SKIP       the function's own copy of `high` advances only when it is called (a = high 6 bars back)
//   CARRY      the copy advances every bar, repeating its last value on bars it is not called
//              (a = high 3 bars back); this is the reading that explains ytc-swing-highs-lows bar 46.
// Read A on the bars where T is 1. B is the same with `[1]` (SKIP and CARRY agree there: control).
// CAPTURE: NYSE:RDDT 1D from the listing. CONTROL for -tern-v1: the same ternary-arm calls at //@version=3 (where the if-block form CARRIES).
study("UCTPROBE_VW_FN_HISTORY_IN_TERN_V3", overlay=false)
f2() => high[2]
f1() => high[1]
t = cum(1) % 3 == 0
a = t ? f2() : 0.0
b = t ? f1() : 0.0
plot(t ? 1 : 0, title="T")
plot(a, title="A_f2_in_if")
plot(b, title="B_f1_in_if")
plot(high[2], title="C_high2_EVERY_BAR")
plot(high[3], title="D_high3_CARRY")
plot(high[6], title="E_high6_SKIP")
plot(cum(1) - 1, title="BI")
